          narration: "So who's who? The Scrum Team has three accountabilities. The Product Owner is accountable for maximising the value of the product. In practice that means owning the Product Backlog: developing and communicating the product goal, creating and clarifying backlog items, and ordering them so the most valuable work comes first. The Product Owner is one person, not a committee, although they may represent many stakeholders. Trust me, a committee ordering a backlog is like five people sharing one steering wheel. The Developers are the people who build the increment each sprint. In Scrum, Developer means anyone doing the work, including testers, designers and analysts. They create the sprint plan, hold each other to the Definition of Done, and decide how to turn backlog items into a working increment. The Scrum Master is accountable for the team's effectiveness and for establishing Scrum as the Scrum Guide defines it. They coach, help remove impediments, and help the wider organisation understand Scrum. A Scrum Master is a servant leader, not a manager handing out tasks. And notice who's missing: there's no project manager role in Scrum. Those responsibilities are spread across the three accountabilities. Yes, I'm trying not to take that personally.",
