          heading: "Artefacts and the Definition of Done",
          narration: "Scrum has three artefacts, and in the 2020 Scrum Guide each one comes with a commitment. Think of the commitment as the thing that keeps the artefact honest. The Product Backlog is the ordered list of everything that might be needed in the product, and its commitment is the Product Goal, the long-term objective the team is working towards. The Sprint Backlog is made up of the sprint goal, the backlog items chosen for the sprint, and the Developers' plan for delivering them. Its commitment is the Sprint Goal, which gives the sprint focus and some flexibility about exactly which items get finished. The Increment is a concrete stepping stone towards the product goal, and its commitment is the Definition of Done. The Definition of Done is a formal description of the quality standards an item must meet to become part of the increment, such as code reviewed, tested and deployed to a staging environment. If an item doesn't meet it, it can't be released or shown off as done at the review. My builder once told me the kitchen was done. There was no sink. A Definition of Done would have saved us both a very awkward conversation.",
