          heading: "Velocity and forecasting",
          narration: "Once a team has a few sprints behind it, it can measure its velocity, the number of story points of done work completed per sprint. Velocity is for forecasting, not for judging. Let's do one together. A team completed twenty two points, then twenty eight, then twenty five in its last three sprints. Add them up: seventy five. Divide by three sprints, and the average velocity is twenty five points. So a sensible forecast for the next sprint is about twenty five points. Notice the word forecast. Not a guarantee, not a ceiling, not a floor. Holidays, illness or one gnarly story can shift it. You can use velocity for release planning too. Two hundred points left in the backlog at twenty five a sprint? Roughly eight sprints. Two classic mistakes. Comparing velocity between teams, which is meaningless because points are relative to each team. And pressuring a team to raise velocity, which just inflates the estimates. Funny how that works. Next week we swing back to the project manager's home turf, integration management: charters, change requests, and how to close a project properly instead of letting it fizzle out. Bring your sense of order.",
