          heading: "What integration management means",
          narration: "Morning, all. Last week was Scrum, with its tidy little roles and events. This week we're back on my home turf: integration management. Of all the knowledge areas, integration belongs most clearly to the project manager. The others, scope, schedule, cost, quality, resources, communications, risk, procurement and stakeholders, each look at one dimension of the project. Integration is about making them all work together. If you shorten the schedule, what happens to cost and risk? If you add a feature, who needs to know, and does the test plan still hold? Integration is the conductor, not any single instrument. In PMBOK terms, its processes are developing the project charter, developing the project management plan, directing and managing project work, managing project knowledge, monitoring and controlling project work, performing integrated change control, and closing the project or phase. You'll notice that list maps neatly onto the five process groups from week one, from initiating through to closing. Today we'll walk through them in order, from the moment a project is officially born to the moment it's formally laid to rest. Yes, projects should actually end. Some of mine needed reminding.",
