          heading: "The project management plan and baselines",
          narration: "Once the project's authorised, planning produces the project management plan. Think of it as the master plan describing how the project will be executed, monitored and controlled, and closed. It pulls together a set of subsidiary plans: scope, schedule, cost, quality, resource, communications, risk, procurement and stakeholder engagement. It also contains the baselines. A baseline is the approved version of a plan that you measure performance against. There are three key ones. The scope baseline, which is the scope statement, the work breakdown structure and its dictionary. The schedule baseline. And the cost baseline. Together they form the performance measurement baseline. And here's the golden rule: baselines only change through formal change control. Why so strict? Because if you quietly move the baseline every time you fall behind, you can never be late. Which sounds lovely, until you realise it also means you can never learn anything from tracking. I once worked with a festival manager who updated the run sheet every time an act ran late, then proudly announced at midnight that we were perfectly on schedule. We were not. A baseline is a promise you measure yourself against, not a moving target.",
