          heading: "Closing the project",
          narration: "Closing is the process group most often rushed, because by the end everyone's exhausted and eyeing the next shiny project. Closing the project or phase means finalising all activities and formally completing the work. The key steps: gain formal acceptance of the deliverables from the customer or sponsor; transfer the product to operations or the customer; close out supplier contracts; release team members and resources; archive project records; and document lessons learned. Formal acceptance matters because without it the project never truly ends. Little requests just keep trickling in, forever. Lessons learned matter because they're how an organisation gets better, instead of repeating the same mistakes with fresh enthusiasm. A good session asks what went well, what didn't, and what we'd do differently, and the answers go into a lessons learned register so the next team can use them. My festival lessons register has one entry in capital letters: confirm the stage delivery date in writing. You'll hear that story properly in a couple of weeks. Projects can also close early when they're cancelled, and they still need proper closure, to document why they stopped and what was learned along the way.",
