          heading: "Collecting requirements",
          narration: "Requirements are the conditions or capabilities a solution must have to satisfy stakeholders. Collecting them well is harder than it sounds. Stakeholders describe solutions instead of needs, they disagree with each other, and they forget things that seem obvious to them. When I asked one festival director what she needed from ticketing, she said a red button. It took three meetings to discover the actual need was getting people through the gate in under ten minutes. Common techniques include interviews, focus groups, facilitated workshops, questionnaires and surveys, observing people doing their current work, prototypes, and analysing existing documents and systems. Each suits different situations. Observation reveals steps people forget to mention; prototypes help people react to something concrete. Requirements are usually classified. Business requirements describe the organisation's high-level needs. Stakeholder requirements describe the needs of particular groups. Solution requirements split into functional ones, describing what the system does, and non-functional ones, describing qualities like performance, security and usability. Transition requirements cover moving from the current state to the future state, such as data conversion and training. And a requirements traceability matrix links each requirement to its source and to the deliverables and tests that satisfy it.",
