          heading: "Variances and performance indices",
          narration: "Now we compare, and every formula starts with earned value. Say it with me: EV first. Cost variance is earned value minus actual cost. Schedule variance is earned value minus planned value. Negative is bad: over budget, or behind schedule. The indices are ratios. The cost performance index, CPI, is earned value divided by actual cost. The schedule performance index, SPI, is earned value divided by planned value. Below one is bad; above one is good. Apply it to our example. Cost variance: fifty thousand minus forty thousand, plus ten thousand, so we're under budget. Schedule variance: fifty thousand minus sixty thousand, minus ten thousand, so we're behind schedule. CPI: fifty divided by forty, one point two five, so we get a dollar twenty five of work for every dollar spent. SPI: fifty divided by sixty, about zero point eight three, so we're moving at about eighty three percent of the planned rate. Mixed results like that are common. A team can look cheap simply because it's understaffed. The classic mistake? Flipping a ratio. Next week: risk management, or how to worry productively. I'm a natural.",
