          heading: "Quantitative analysis and expected monetary value",
          narration: "For the highest priority risks, or on big projects, quantitative analysis puts actual numbers on the effect. The key technique for us is expected monetary value, or EMV. The EMV of a risk is its probability multiplied by its impact. Threats have negative impacts; opportunities have positive ones. Let's do some. There's a thirty percent chance a supplier delivers hardware late, which would cost forty thousand dollars in delays. EMV is zero point three times forty thousand: twelve thousand dollars of expected cost. Now an opportunity: a twenty five percent chance of a licensing discount worth twenty thousand dollars. EMV is positive five thousand. Here's the subtle bit. EMV doesn't predict what will happen. The supplier is either late or not. It tells you the average effect if you faced that situation many times, which makes it useful for comparing options and sizing the contingency reserve. You can extend it into a decision tree. Option A costs fifty thousand, with a ten percent chance of a hundred thousand dollars of rework, so its expected cost is sixty thousand. Option B costs fifty five thousand with no rework risk. B wins, even though its sticker price is higher.",
